				\begin{document}$${{\text{E}}_{{\text{ads}}}}{\text{= }}{{\text{E}}_{{\text{Belzutifan@SiC}}}}\:-\:\left( {{\text{ }}{{\text{E}}_{{\text{Belzutifan}}}}{\text{+ }}{{\text{E}}_{{\text{SiC}}}}} \right)$$\end{document} with basis set superposition error (BSSE) corrected via the counterpoise method 26  to account for artificial energy lowering from incomplete basis sets – a critical adjustment that typically reduces overestimation of adsorption energies by 15–20% in such systems 34 – 36 . Geometry optimization and frequency analysis ensured the system reached a true local minimum on the potential energy surface, with convergence criteria of 10⁻⁶ Ha/atom for energy and 0.01 eV/Å for forces. The reliability of our DFT approach was validated by excellent agreement with experimental data: calculated SiC bulk modulus (220 GPa) deviates by < 2% from measured values (224 ± 5 GPa) 37 , while Belzutifan vibrational frequencies show < 5% deviation from experimental IR spectra. Complementary MD simulations were conducted using OpenMX’s which reproduces DFT-level accuracy for bond formation/breaking while including explicit polarization effects essential for modeling drug-surface interactions. Simulations employed an NVT ensemble at 310 K (Langevin thermostat, γ = 1 ps⁻¹) to approximate physiological conditions, with a 1 fs time step verified by energy conservation tests and 5 ps duration sufficient for system equilibration as evidenced by RMSD plateauing after 2 ps. Trajectory analysis incorporated root mean square deviation (RMSD) calculations using backbone atoms, hydrogen bond identification (distance < 3.5 Å, angle > 150°), and charge transfer evaluation via Mulliken population analysis. Key assumptions include rigid nanocrystal surfaces and neutral pH conditions. Future work will incorporate surface defects and pH-dependent charge effects to better approximate biological environments.

