Results and discussion Figure  1  presents the molecular configuration of Belzutifan, modeled using DFT. The left section of the figure contains a structural diagram that highlights the connectivity between the various atoms, featuring critical functional groups such as sulfonyl and fluorinated moieties. The right section depicts optimized three-dimensional representation of the molecule, showcasing its spatial arrangement and geometric properties. The figure illustrates the Molecular Electrostatic Potential (MEP) of Belzutifan, with color coding that highlights the distribution of electrostatic potential across the molecule. Red areas indicate regions of negative electrostatic potential, suggesting high electron density and potential sites for electrophilic interactions, while blue areas represent positive electrostatic potential, indicating potential interactions with nucleophiles. Green regions denote areas of neutral electrostatic potential, where interactions are less pronounced. The molecular structure of Belzutifan is depicted alongside its functional groups, such as fluorine, sulfur, and oxygen, emphasizing their arrangement and contribution to the molecule’s electrostatic properties. 
 Fig. 1 The structural and 3D representation of molecular configurations and MEP of Belzutifan. 
 Table  1  provides detailed quantitative data on the distances between atomic pairs and the angles formed by specific bonds within the Belzutifan molecule. The bond lengths reveal that the S-O bond (1.45 Å) is shorter than the S-C bond (1.74 Å), indicating a stronger interaction between sulfur and oxygen. The C-N bond (1.16 Å) is notably the shortest, suggesting a strong single bond between carbon and nitrogen, while the F-C bond (1.36 Å) also reflects a robust connection. This variation in bond lengths provides insight into the relative strengths and types of interactions within the molecule. Bond angles further illustrate the molecular geometry, with the O-S-C angle measuring 107.16° and the F-C-O angle at 108.28°. Both angles are close to the ideal tetrahedral angle of 109.5°, indicating sp³ hybridization around the sulfur and carbon atoms. 
