Conclusion This computational study demonstrates that carboxyl-functionalized silicon carbide (SiC-COOH) nanocrystals exhibit superior performance as nanocarriers for Belzutifan delivery, achieving a high adsorption energy of − 1.03 eV—25% stronger than conventional carbon-based carriers. The SiC-COOH system’s exceptional stability (formation energy: −5.42 eV/atom) and pronounced polarity (dipole moment: 142.1 D) facilitate both robust drug binding and enhanced solubility. Electronic structure analysis reveals critical charge transfers (− 0.25e) from Belzutifan to the nanocrystal, accompanied by a significant reduction in the HOMO-LUMO gap from 5.427 eV (pristine SiC) to 3.41 eV (Belzutifan@SiC-COOH), which enhances electronic coupling and suggests potential for photo-triggered release. Molecular dynamics simulations confirm the complex’s stability under physiological conditions, with structural deviations (RMSD) remaining below 2.5 Å over 5 ps, while spectroscopic shifts in the 200–600 nm range and IR vibrational changes (Δν = 15–40 cm⁻¹) provide detectable signatures of successful adsorption. The dominance of hydrogen bonding and van der Waals interactions, as evidenced by reduced density gradient analysis, underpins the system’s stability. These findings position SiC-COOH nanocrystals as a promising platform for targeted Belzutifan delivery, addressing key challenges in solubility and controlled release for HIF inhibitor therapies. The insights gained from this work not only advance the design of ceramic-based nanocarriers but also establish a computational framework for optimizing drug-nanoparticle interactions in oncology applications.
